A||||2||cmyokley||master cylinder problem?||||8
Z||000000||cmyokley||09-25-1999||01:30 PM||||I was having a problem with my front right brake dragging. In all my infinite wisdom and reading the mananul I decided that since there was black residue in the reservior I had somehow contaminated my brake system. I decided that I would replace the master cylinder and bleed the system back down. I also decided that I would tear the right front caliper off, again, and give it a once over.<P>Since the brake pads were dragging very hard on the rotor I decided to pull the piston and see what was up. I could not push the piston out with air so I used the cars hydraulic system and pumped the piston out. I removed the dust cover and piston seal. Then I tried to re-insert the piston. It would only go in half way. I saw a scuff mark on the piston at the same place it would stop at. This small mark on the piston was keeping it from moving all the way in and was also keeping it from coming out. I had an old set of calipers, so I got the piston from them, cleaned it up and it slid in just like it was suppose to. Great I thought.<P>Since I had purchased a new (rebuilt) master cylinder I decided to go ahead and install it. I did and now comes the funny part.<P>I can not get all the air out of the system. The front I can bleed fine, but the rear calipers I can not get the air out of. In fact it is like I am pumping air into the system. Every time I push the pedal down I get half air and half brake fluid, every time.<P>Is it possible that the rebuilt master cylinder is defective? Would I have done better by rebuilding my old master cylinder? <P>It is also interesting to note that as I <BR>open the front caliper bleeder port the brake pedal goes all the way to the floor, and as I open the bleeder port to both the rear caliper bleeder ports the brake padel does not go to the floor. What in the heck caused this funny problem.<P>Anyone have any idea what is going on.<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Dan||09-25-1999||06:31 PM|||| No problem. All small GM cars require bench bleeding of master cylinders prior to installation.Remove your master cylinder and put it in a vice.Top off level with fluid. Slowly,carefully push in the piston from the back and watch all those little air bubbles rise.Pump it in and out several times.For some reason one side will easily bleed without bench bleeding.I can't remember but<BR>you may need some plugs to block off the hydraulic line comming out of the master in an action like bleeding at the wheel cylinder.For sure it needs to be bled and full prior to installation.Anyone who has spongy brakes or low pedal should try at the wheel cylinders first and may end up bench bleeding the master.Always ensure you don't go low on fluid when bleeding.||||reg||10
Z||000002||cmyokley||09-27-1999||12:34 PM||||Dan, thanks for the reply.<P>What I found out was that the new "rebuilt" master cylinder was defective. I got so mad while trying to bleed the system I pulled the new "rebuilt" master cylinder off, re-bleed, and re-installed. I had the same problem and could not bleed the rear brakes. I then dismantled the "old" master cylinder, cleaned it up and re-installed it....bingo...I could then bleed the brakes just like always. BAD RE-BUILT PARTS FROM HI-LO. I guess I should learn my lesson and never buy rebuilt parts.....I am assuming that there is a bad seal in the new part. I will be having a heart to heart talk with the parts dealer. I spent six hours in the process.||||reg||9
